00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:57.120
Hello everyone. I am in Kang from Soul

00:03:54.959 --> 00:04:00.159
National University. Today I will

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:03.519
present NVME next a virtual NVME device

00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:06.000
for GPUcentric storage systems.

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:09.040
As modern GPU workloads have outgrown

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:11.200
HBM, recent systems offload data sets to

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:14.480
the storage tier. But conventionally,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:17.120
the CPU sits in the middle of every GPU

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:19.680
storage transfer and advine granularity,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:22.960
but that becomes the bottleneck. GPU

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.680
initiated IO removes it. GPU threads

00:04:22.960 --> 00:04:28.320
issue IO's directly with no GPU

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:30.720
intervention. The industry is moving the

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.800
same direction. Nvidia's storage next

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.440
vision redefineses storage as an

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:38.080
extension of GPU memory for both

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.560
performance and cost. But the devices

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:43.040
this vision assumes do not exist yet.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.440
That is why we need a scalable emulator

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.759
to scale under extreme concurrency and

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:50.160
million IOPS class throughput demands

00:04:47.759 --> 00:04:53.360
and to perform efficient P-2e

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:57.120
communication with GPUs. This is exactly

00:04:53.360 --> 00:05:00.880
the gap NVME next fills.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.680
We built NVME next on top of our prior

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.400
emulator NVME vert rearchitecting around

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.039
two elements. First, a multim manager

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:12.240
architecture. A manager is the basic

00:05:09.039 --> 00:05:14.479
operating unit of NVME next performing

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:17.280
the entire sequence from fetching NVME

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:19.680
commands to posting completions. NVM

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:22.000
next charge NVME Q pairs across

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:25.039
managers. So each manager owns a

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.759
disjoint set of cues. This parallelizes

00:05:25.039 --> 00:05:30.240
request processing with no inter thread

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:33.199
synchronization letting the emulator

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.360
scale under extreme concurrency.

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:38.160
Second, the performance model. It

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:41.360
captures NAND read latency along with

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:44.320
PCIe and NAND channel occupancy because

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.800
every manager shares this model. Locking

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:49.120
becomes a critical design point. A

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:51.600
global lock causes contention. Per

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:54.560
manager partitioning avoids that but it

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:57.440
is inaccurate for mixed workloads. Under

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:00.160
small IOS NAND read latency dominates

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.720
while channel and PCIe bandwidth save

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:05.440
far from saturation. So NBME next

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.000
assigns each transfer of precomputed

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:10.720
latency instead of modeling channel

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:13.919
contention. Then managers only need to

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:18.160
share each plane status since NAND

00:06:13.919 --> 00:06:21.869
operations serialize per plane.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.080
Each manager operates in three phase

00:06:21.869 --> 00:06:26.800
[snorts] Each manager operates in three

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.600
phases. Phase one is command fetching.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:32.560
The manager scans the SQ door burst then

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:35.520
copies the SQ entries from GPU memory

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:38.560
into its entry buffers. This copy is

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:42.720
offloaded to Intel DSA batching entries

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:45.280
from multiple SQS at once.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.440
Phase two is data processing. The

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:49.199
manager consults the performance model

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:51.840
to compute each command's target

00:06:49.199 --> 00:06:55.360
completion time. The manager then issues

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.639
multiple transfers as a single DSA batch

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:01.199
at 512 bytes. The per request setup

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.759
overhead is no longer negligible

00:07:01.199 --> 00:07:08.160
relative to the payload and batching

00:07:03.759 --> 00:07:10.639
amortizes it across many requests.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.520
Phase three is completion handling. Once

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:16.000
the target completion time has elapsed,

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.440
the manager collects the CQ entries into

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:23.199
the the entry buffer and posts them to

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:27.680
the CQ in GPU memory.

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:30.639
All experiments run on an A100 GPU. We

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:33.360
configured the model with 16 N channels,

00:07:30.639 --> 00:07:36.160
32 planes per channel, and a 15

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.599
microscond N read latency, giving a

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:42.560
theoretical peak of 33 million IOPS. On

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:44.880
the left, we run a 512 byte random read

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:47.440
workload while varying the number of Q

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.680
pairs and the per Q depth. Notice that

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:52.080
the same total Q depth gives different

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.360
throughput depending on the topology.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:57.919
Sharing a Q pair across more GPU threads

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:00.879
increases synchronization overhead.

00:07:57.919 --> 00:08:03.199
Panel B applies warp level colossing

00:08:00.879 --> 00:08:05.440
where the lane zero thread performs the

00:08:03.199 --> 00:08:08.960
critical section work for the whole

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:11.759
warp. Most configurations improve with

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:14.400
the best case scanning 2x. Notably,

00:08:11.759 --> 00:08:17.280
NVMENX reaches the theoretical peak

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:19.759
under a real GPU workload.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.479
On the right, BFS and connected

00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:25.120
components over the orut and model data

00:08:22.479 --> 00:08:27.840
sets. By default, one GPU thread per

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.560
vertex scans all of its edges. One

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:32.880
optimization is edge polishing where a

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.680
single warp handles the edges of one

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:40.080
vertex together. This changes execution

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:42.719
time dramatically. BFS and on or runs 3x

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:46.240
faster. But look at the third chart on

00:08:42.719 --> 00:08:48.640
MVME. The same optim optimization barely

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:51.680
moves the execution time when the

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:54.640
emulator itself is the bottleneck. GPU

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:57.279
side optimizations become invisible.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.600
NVME next scales enough to let them

00:08:57.279 --> 00:09:03.360
show.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:06.560
To summarize, NVME next is an NVME SSD

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:09.200
emulator for GPUcentric storage systems.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:11.920
It handles requests at million IOPS

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:15.839
scale and works as a tool for system

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:18.480
level analysis. MVM next is open source

00:09:15.839 --> 00:09:24.360
and we hope it's useful for future

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:24.360
GPUcentric storage research. Thank you.

